<!--/* In the name of God, the Merciful, the Compassionate */-->
@using SQLTriage.Data.Services
@inject AppUserState UserState

@* ShellGate — a SECOND-LAYER authorization check on the always-rendered app shell.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

   ⚠ IT IS NO LONGER THE BOUNDARY. It was, for round 7, and it lasted one round: a cold gate beat
   it with two shapes that are not adversarial at all — <input @bind:get @bind:set /> (there is no
   @bind= attribute to match) and <EditForm OnValidSubmit> with a submit button (there is no <form>
   tag and no repo-declared EventCallback). Both were green on the census and both FIRED LIVE from
   http://192.10.10.32/scheduled-tasks as an unauthenticated viewer.

   The boundary now lives in the HTTP pipeline: Data/Services/InteractiveAppAdmission.cs refuses
   the interactive application itself — document, static assets and the /_blazor circuit — to an
   unauthenticated caller from a non-loopback origin. Nothing that never receives a circuit can
   drive a control, whatever the markup looks like.

   Keep this component. It is still the thing that decides what a SIGNED-IN caller who lacks a
   permission may see, and it is still the reason a viewer on the LAN gets the shell's text and not
   its switches. It is defence in depth now, and read it that way: a hole HERE is a privilege
   escalation between authenticated roles, not an anonymous compromise, and a control that slips
   past the census is no longer an exploit on its own.

   The rest of this comment is round 7's reasoning, kept because it is still why the component has
   the shape it has.

   One component. It evaluates the permission and renders its ChildContent only when the
   circuit is authorized. A denied caller gets the shell's TEXT and never its SWITCHES.

   WHY A BOUNDARY AND NOT A PER-CONTROL @if.
   Rounds 4, 5 and 6 each shipped a census that decided whether a shell control was safe by
   looking at WHAT THE HANDLER CALLS, and each was defeated by a shape the scanner could not
   see: a verb outside the lexicon (SetAnonymiseServerNames), a call outside the prefix anchor
   (ShortcutSvc.TriggerRun), and finally a receiver alias —

       P1   UserSettings.NudgeReportOperator("...")                 → census RED
       P2   var s = UserSettings; s.NudgeReportOperator("...")       → census GREEN, exploited
                                                                       live from the LAN denial
                                                                       page at 192.10.10.32:5300

   One extra line of C# defeated the instrument. Chasing that leads to lambdas, method groups,
   interface indirection, reflection and DynamicInvoke, and a source scanner asking "what does
   this handler call?" loses that race by construction.

   So the question changed. This component exists so the census can ask a MARKUP question the
   call cannot influence: is this interactive element inside an authorization boundary? P1 and
   P2 are byte-identical in markup, so they now fail — or pass — identically.

   The decider is RbacShellBoundaryCensusTests. Wrapping markup in this component is the ONLY
   way a shell control gets to render to a caller who might not be an admin; the alternative is
   an entry on ShellSurfaceRegistry.InertControls, which is a written claim in test code that
   the control touches nothing but this circuit's own view.

   PLAIN IsAuthorized, by round 3's ruling: break-glass is scoped to Settings and Onboarding.
   The one shell control that legitimately carries it — the top-bar Settings shortcut, the
   console route into the page that can undo a bad RBAC configuration — sets BreakGlass and
   must be listed on ShellSurfaceRegistry.BreakGlassBoundaries with its reason.

   Renders NOTHING when denied. It deliberately does not fall back to the AccessDenied panel the
   way RbacGuard does: this wraps chrome that sits on every route, and a shell that sprayed denial
   panels across the status bar and nav would be unusable. RbacGuard stays the right component
   for a PAGE region where the denial should be visible.

   (The component name above is written in prose rather than as a tag on purpose. The shell set is
   DERIVED by walking component tags out of MainLayout, and that walk reads the raw file — so a tag
   written inside a comment pulls a component that nothing renders into the shell closure.) *@

@if (Permitted)
{
    @ChildContent
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// RBAC permission key. Evaluated through <see cref="AppUserState.IsAuthorized(string)"/>,
    /// which is the same decision as <c>RbacService</c> plus the one thing the static matrix
    /// cannot know: whether this caller is entitled to the unconfigured-install bootstrap hatch
    /// at all. On a network-reachable listener "unconfigured" must not mean "admin for anyone
    /// who can route to the port".
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Permission { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>The markup rendered only to an authorized circuit.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Opt in to round 1's break-glass hatch. Scoped by ruling to Settings and Onboarding, and
    /// every use in a shell file must be named on
    /// <c>ShellSurfaceRegistry.BreakGlassBoundaries</c> with the reason it is one of those two.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool BreakGlass { get; set; } = false;

    /// <summary>
    /// Evaluated in the render body rather than cached in OnParametersSet, so a re-render of the
    /// host always re-asks. A cached decision is a decision that can go stale against
    /// <see cref="AppUserState"/>'s TTL refresh.
    /// </summary>
    private bool Permitted =>
        UserState != null
        && (BreakGlass ? UserState.IsAuthorizedWithBreakGlass(Permission)
                       : UserState.IsAuthorized(Permission));
}
